  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Friday/...\nOn Tuesday, an upper low will slowly weaken as it moves northeast \ninto NV/UT before getting swept up into the large scale flow on \nWednesday moving into the northern Plains. At the same time, a weak \nridge over TX and will start to strengthen on resulting in \nsouthwesterly flow over our area. With moisture return and some \nupper level forcing from the aforementioned low, rain chances will \nreturn to the higher terrain areas in northern and eastern Arizona. \nWeak large scale troughing will remain across the eastern \nPacific/West Coast through the end of next week, battling the ridge \nof high pressure over TX. By the end of the week, the ridge will \ntry to retrograde into NM/AZ. Additionally, ensemble members bring a \nsurge of moisture into our area from the SE, pushing PWATs back into \na 1.0-1.3\" range. As a result, NBM/WPC PoPs for the end of next week \nreflect a 30-50% chance of showers and storms across the higher \nterrain in southern Gila County, and around 10-20% across the lower \ndeserts of south-central Arizona.\n\nTemperatures next week will remain largely unchanged in the upper \n90s to low 100s across the lower deserts, and in the upper 80s to \nmid 90s across the higher terrain. Morning lows will generally be \nin the 70s to to low 80s across the lower deserts, and in the 60s \nacross the higher terrain areas. Morning lows should start to creep \nup by the end of next week (more morning lows in the low 80s across \nthe lower deserts). As a result, widespread minor HeatRisk will \ncontinue through the beginning of next week with moderate HeatRisk \nreturning to the lower deserts for the latter half of next week as \nthe morning lows creep up and daily normals decrease.",
